Figure 2: (a) BF TEM image of as-cast Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{48}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\), viewed along [100] of the B2 phase, and (b,c) CBED patterns from the two phases. Note the (001) diffraction spot in (b) indicates the ordered nature of this phase; (d) BF TEM image and (e) CBED pattern of the grain boundary phase in as-cast Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\).

The width of both phases after the 0.25 h anneal had increased from 50 nm to \(\sim\)350 nm. Fig. 4(b-j) shows SE images of Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{23}\)Al\({}_{21}\) annealed at 1173 K for additional times up to 250 h, at which time the phase width was \(\sim\)2.5 um. All of them exhibited lamellar-like structure with increasing phase widths as the annealing time increased.

The increase in phase size is plotted on Fig. 5 according to the relationship:

\[r^{2}-r_{o}^{2}=kt \tag{1}\]

on log scales, where \(r\) is the phase width at time \(t\) at 1173 K, \(r_{o}\) is initial phase width, and \(k\) is a constant, and shows a good linear fit (correlation coefficient \(R^{2}=0.97\)).

EDS data for the individual phases, obtained from thin foils in the TEM, are shown in Table 1 for the as-cast condition and after annealing at 1173 K for 20 h, 100 h and 250 h. For all cases, the B2 phase is rich in Ni and Al, while the fcc phase is rich in Fe and Mn. The grain boundary phase has a composition similar to that of the matrix fcc phase. These observations are consistent with the fact that Mn is an fcc stabilizer in iron-rich phases, while Al is a bcc stabilizer [1,20]. It is interesting to note that after annealing, the B2 phase contains more Fe and Mn, and less Ni and Al than the as-cast material.

There is a clear change in chemistry between the as-cast material and the annealed specimens. This is presumably because the phases in the as-cast material reflect the temperature at which they are formed, while the annealed specimens reflect the equilibrium phase compositions at the temperature of the anneal. For the annealed specimens, it is worth noting that with increasing annealing time, the Fe and Al contents of the fcc phase decrease while the Ni and Mn contents increase with annealing time.

### Mechanical properties

The hardness of as-cast Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{23}\)Al\({}_{21}\) was measured to be \(467\pm 18\) HV, a value comparable to that of the similar alloy Fe\({}_{20}\)Ni\({}_{20}\)Mn\({}_{20}\)Al\({}_{20}\) at \(454\pm 6\) HV noted earlier [1].

Nanoinindentation was performed on the alloy after annealing for 100 h at 1173 K, which was used to coarsen the phases. This gave values of the hardness of \(5.0\pm 0.3\) GPa for the fcc phase and \(7.3\pm 0.2\) GPa for the B2, i.e. the B2 phase is significantly harder than the fcc phase. Similar results were observed for a fcc/B2 lamellar-structured eutectic FeNiMnAl alloy [6].

As-cast Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{23}\)Al\({}_{21}\) was annealed at 573 K for 2 h to reduce any as-cast residual stresses. Tensile tests at room temperature at a strain rate of \(5\times 10^{-4}\) s\({}^{-1}\) were performed on both the as-cast material and after the 573 K anneal. All specimens fractured before any sign of plastic deformation showing failure by transgranular fracture, see Fig. 6(a,b).

In order to obtain a yield strength for the as-cast material, compression tests were also performed on the as-cast Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{23}\)Al\({}_{21}\) at a strain rate of \(5\times 10^{-4}\) s\({}^{-1}\). The yield stress of the as-cast Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{23}\)Al\({}_{21}\) in compression is \(\sim\)1180 \(\pm\) 30 MPa (average of three tests), which is higher than that of fcc/B2 two-phase lamellar-structured Fe\({}_{20}\)Ni\({}_{20}\)Mn\({}_{23}\)Al\({}_{15}\) at \(\sim\)800 MPa [5].

Figure 3: XRD patterns from bulk Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{23}\)Al\({}_{21}\) annealed at 1173 K for 1 h followed by quenching into water and for 20 h followed by cooling in air. Both show only B2 and fcc peaks.

Room temperature tensile tests at an initial strain rate of \(5\times 10^{-4}\) s\({}^{-1}\) were performed on Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{32}\)Al\({}_{21}\) annealed at 1173 K for various times up to 250 h corresponding to the microstructures shown in Fig. 4. Three tests were performed for each heat treatment condition. Fig. 7 shows typical stress strain curves for each annealing time, where it is evident that as the annealing time increased, i.e. as the microstructure became coarser, the yield strength, work-hardening rate and fracture strength decreased, while the ductility increased.

A more ductile fracture mode was found in the annealed Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\), see for example Fig. 6(c,d), which is consistent with the increased elongation compared to that of the as-cast Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\).

TEM examination of Fe\({}_{28}\)Ni\({}_{18}\)Mn\({}_{33}\)Al\({}_{21}\) annealed for 100 h at 1173 K and strained to failure (\(\sim\)7% elongation) showed that dislocations were present in both the fcc and B2 phases, see Fig. 8, indicating that both phases underwent plastic deformation.

