## 4 Discussion

### Lamellar coarsening

Particle coarsening kinetics are controlled particularly at high temperatures, by lattice diffusion which can often be described by the Lifshitz, Slyozov and Wagner (LSW) theory [21, 22] where the particle radius \(r\), increases with increasing time, \(t\), according to

\[r^{3}-r_{o}^{3}=kt \tag{2}\]

where \(r_{o}\) is the initial particle size and the constant, \(k\), depends on a variety of parameters including the interfacial free energy and the chemical diffusion coefficient among others. Since a plot of Eq. (2) only has a correlation coefficient, \(R^{2}\), of 0.94 versus a \(R^{2}\) of 0.97 for a plot of \(r^{2}-r_{o}^{2}=kt\) (Fig. 5), the use of the cubic exponent is not immediately compelling. The lack of agreement with ISW theory is perhaps not surprising since that theory was derived for spherical precipitates with a zero volume fraction in a supersaturated matrix.

A model that may be more relevant to the current situation is that developed by Ardell and Ozolins [23] and later by Ardell [24, 25, 26] which is based on trans-interface, diffusion-controlled coarsening between ordered and disordered phases with coherent interfaces. This model, which was developed for L1\({}_{2}\)-structured (ordered fcc) Ni\({}_{3}\)Al precipitates in a disordered Ni-Al matrix, predicts that the average particle size (\(r\)) growth can be described by \(\langle r\rangle^{n}\propto t\), where \(n=2.4\). Ardell [25] reanalyzed data for a number of alloys and showed that in some cases \(R^{2}\) was greater for plots of \(\langle r\rangle^{2.4}\propto t\) than for the more traditional \(\langle r\rangle^{3}\propto t\)[21, 22]. The differences in correlation coefficient were often quite small.

Following the approach of Ardell [25], \(r^{n}\) was plotted versus \(t\) for several values of \(n\) between 2 and 3, and the correlation coefficient \(R^{2}\) was determined. A plot of \(R^{2}\) versus \(n\) is shown in Fig. 9. It is evident that \(n=2\) has the highest correlation coefficient and \(n=3\) has the lowest, although it is worth noting that the range of \(R^{2}\) values is only 0.94-0.97. As noted by Ardell [25], picking the exponent \(n\) based on the correlation coefficient alone is not a good approach. Thus, we note here that even though \(n=2\) has the highest \(R^{2}\) value, \(n\) values from 2-3 could reasonably be used to describe the coarsening data.

### Yield strength

Fig. 10 shows plots of the yield strength, the fracture stress and the work-hardening rate measured at 1% strain versus \(\dot{\lambda}^{-1/2}\), where \(\dot{\lambda}\) is the fcc lamellae spacing. The reason for plotting these parameters against some parameterization of \(\dot{\lambda}\) is that previous research has shown that in eutectic fcc/B2 FeNiMnAl alloys the B2 phase is much stronger than the fcc phase at room temperature. Thus, _initial_ plastic deformation occurs _only_ in the fcc. Hence, the parameter controlling the strength is \(\dot{\lambda}\). Interestingly, once plastic deformation occurs, the fcc phase work hardens and its flow strength can reach the yield strength of the B2 phase, at which point plastic deformation occurs in both phases. This is consistent with the observation of dislocations in both phases of the annealed material that shows significant ductility when strained to fracture (Fig. 8).

For most metallic materials [27], the yield strength, \(\sigma_{p}\), is related to the grain size, \(d\), though the Hall-Petch relationship [28, 29], i.e.

\[\sigma_{y}=\sigma_{o}+k_{y}d^{-1/2} \tag{3}\]

where \(\sigma_{o}\) is the lattice resistance and \(k_{y}\) is a constant. The relationship is generally applicable well into the nanocrystalline regime [30, 31]. A Hall-Petch relationship also describes the yield strength of nanolaminates in which layers of two different materials are present [32].

In the present work, a Hall-Petch-type plot (Fig. 10) of the yield strength versus \(\dot{\lambda}^{-1/2}\) has a correlation coefficient \(R^{2}\) of 0.93, suggesting the relationship works well over the two orders of magnitude change in \(\dot{\lambda}\) studied here. Thus, the yield stress of the alloy is consistent with the stress necessary to move dislocations in the fcc phase, which increases with interlamellar spacing refinement [16].

It is of interest to examine whether this relationship is observed in other lamellar materials. Previous work on the eutectic alloy Fe\({}_{30}\)Ni\({}_{20}\)Mn\({}_{35}\)Al\({}_{15}\) showed a \(\dot{\lambda}^{-1/2}\) dependence of the hardness (which is equivalent not to the yield strength but to the flow stress at a strain of \(\sim\)8%), but over a shorter range of \(\dot{\lambda}\) values (approximately one order of magnitude), and for generally larger lamellae sizes (0.5-10 um) than those studied here. A \(\dot{\lambda}^{-1/2}\) dependence of the yield strength has also observed in hot-extended duplex TiAl, which consists of \(\gamma\)-TiAl (L1\({}_{0}\) structure) and \(\sigma_{2}\) -TiAl (DO\({}_{19}\) structure) phases, over ranges of lamellar sizes ranging from 30 to 400 nm [33, 34] and from 20-590 nm [12], in directionally-solidified crystals of TiAl with lamellar spacing of 0.37-1.8 um [10], and in two-phase Fe-Mn-Al-C alloy over a \(\dot{\lambda}\) range of 60-500 nm [35]. Similarly, a \(\dot{\lambda}^{-1/2}\) dependence of the yield strength is a reasonable fit for various eutectold and ultrahigh carbon steels [15, 36], although the range of lamellar spacings studied was often quite small, e.g. 125-225 nm [36].

In contrast, a number of researchers [16, 37, 38, 39] have found that the relationship \(\sigma_{y}=\sigma_{o}+k_{y}\dot{\lambda}^{-1}\) was a better fit for the yield strength in a number of penartic steels in which the onset of yielding occurred in the ferrite. Similarly, \(\dot{\lambda}^{-1}\) is also a better description for the yield strength for _eutectic_ FeNiMnAl alloys over a range of Al contents [17] in which initial deformation occurs in the fcc phase. For the latter materials [16, 17, 37, 38, 39] a plot of \(\sigma_{y}\) versus \(\dot{\lambda}^{-1/2}\) produces a non-physical, negative value for \(\sigma_{o}\). It is worth noting that the range of \(\dot{\lambda}\) values examined for the steels and the eutectic FeNiMnAl alloys [16, 17, 37, 38, 39] was much less than that examined for the materials noted above that showed a \(\dot{\lambda}^{-1/2}\) dependence. Recently, Armstrong [40, 41] has argued that the observed change from an exponent of \(-1/2\) to \(-1\) reflects a change in deformation mechanism from having pile-up at a grain boundary to having a single dislocation expand against a grain boundary.

