### Work-hardening Rate

The Hall-Petch relationship has been extended to describe the relationship between the flow stress, \(\sigma(\varepsilon)\), and the grain size at some strain, \(\varepsilon\), i.e.

\[\sigma(\varepsilon)=\sigma_{\circ}(\varepsilon)+k(\varepsilon)d^{-1/2} \tag{4}\]

where the constants \(\sigma_{\circ}(\varepsilon)\) and \(k(\varepsilon)\) depend on the strain [27, 36, 42]. Thus, plots of the room temperature flow stress measured at different strains versus \(d^{1/2}\) produced straight lines in aluminum [43] and copper [44] among other materials [27], at least up to strains of 20%.

The effect of grain size on the work-hardening rate, defined as:

\[\theta=\frac{d\sigma}{dz} \tag{5}\]

has received much less attention [36], Kovacs, Chinh, and Kovacs-Cestenyi [36] developed a phenomenological model for the dependence of \(\theta\) on grain size and compared the resulting constitutive equations to the work-hardening behavior of 99.99% aluminum strained under tension. They showed that at low strains (\(\leq\)10%), \(\theta\) could be related to the grain size over the grain size range of 40-300 um by

\[\theta\propto d^{-1/2} \tag{6}\]

In contrast, at large strains \(\theta\) becomes independent of the grain size. In other words, at low strains the deformation within grains is strongly affected by the deformation at the grain boundaries, whereas at larger strains, when the density of statistically stored dislocations is higher, the deformation in the grains becomes independent of the deformation occurring at the grain boundaries.

The work here shows a similar relationship between \(\theta\), measured at 1% strain, and \(\lambda^{-1/2}\) over a range of \(\lambda\) of two orders of magnitude from 50 nm to 2.5 um (\(R^{2}=0.82\)) to that observed by Kovacs, Chinh, and Kovacs-Cestenyi [36] in aluminum, i.e.

\[\theta\propto\lambda^{-1/2} \tag{7}\]

There are very little data on the effect of lamellar spacing on \(\theta\). However, in contrast to the observations here, Dollar et al. [16] reported that \(\theta\) in the pearmitic steels that they studied was not affected by \(\dot{\nu}\) over the range of 0.13-0.53 um. Similarly, Karlsson and Linden [39] reported that the work-hardening rate was independent of the scale of the microstructure for both pearlite and spheroidite.

### Ductility

Prior research on the lamellar eutectic fcc/B2 alloy Fe\({}_{20}\)Ni\({}_{20}\)Mn\({}_{35}\)Al\({}_{15}\) showed that while initial plastic deformation occurred in the fcc phase, fracture was initiated in the B2 phase,

